WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.550
DIRE STATE:
Once upon a time, Panasonic was one of the

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:08.340
largest electronics brands in the world making
everything from TVs and appliances to batteries

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.599
and phones.

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:14.389
They were the pride of Japan alongside Sony
and Toshiba throughout the mid to late 1900s

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.890
as they recovered for the devastating ruins
of World War 2.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:22.900
But, the 2000s haven’t been so nice to Panasonic.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.580
They’ve shut down their communications business,
much of their TV business, and even their

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.050
LCD business.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.380
They’ve been forced to sell off many of
their assets including their security business,

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:38.520
their semiconductor business, and most notably
their entire $3.5 billion stake in Tesla.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.870
Things haven’t been looking much better
internally either.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:47.629
Panasonic’s revenue has fallen by over 40%,
their gross profit has nearly halved, and

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:50.789
they’ve written off tens of billions of
dollars worth of losses.

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:55.329
As such, their employee count has shrunk by
over a hundred thousand people within the

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:57.320
past 13 years.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.739
Honestly, the only thing at Panasonic that
hasn’t gone straight down is ironically

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:03.090
their stock price.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.119
But this isn’t all that much better anyway
as the stock has basically just been going

00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:09.400
sideways for the past 30 years.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:14.049
And, when you account for inflation, you’ll
see that Panasonic stock is actually down

00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:17.210
66% over the past 30 years.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:21.840
Seeing all this, I decided to check the bankruptcy
probability for Panasonic.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.630
Usually for big tech companies, the probability
is less than 1%.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:30.480
For more distressed companies like GoPro,
the number is usually in the low double digits.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.229
So, with that in mind, do you wanna guess
how much it is for Panasonic?

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:37.450
Maybe 70 or 80%?

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.909
Well, it’s straight up over 100%.

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:45.469
This is something that I’ve never seen before.

00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:50.610
So, odds are that unless a miracle happens
or the Japanese government steps, someone

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:55.290
who’s already drowning in debt, Panasonic
is as good as dead.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.689
But what happened?

00:01:57.689 --> 00:02:01.540
As far as I can remember, there was never
really any pivotal moment for the company.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:05.460
They kind of just slowly lost steam over the
course of decades right?

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.740
Well, there’s actually a lot more to the
story as much of Panasonic’s demise can

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.890
be attributed to them forgetting about their
roots.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:19.750
So, join me as take a look back at the rise
and fall of Panasonic and try to pinpoint

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:23.130
what went wrong.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:29.690
ALWAYS KEEP EXPERIMENTING:
Panasonic's fall from grace shows how even

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:35.160
major corporations can falter when they lose
sight of their core strengths.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.120
This iconic Japanese brand went from electronics
giant to borderline bankruptcy by failing

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.480
to adapt to changing technologies and markets.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.210
Now, everyday investors face their own challenges
finding stable returns amidst economic uncertainty.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:55.590
Just last week, the Federal Reserve opted
