00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:48.530
But anyway, taking a look back, the story
of Panasonic dates back to November 27, 1894

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.470
to a man named Konosuke Matsushita.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:57.819
Konosuke was born to a poor rice farming family
and was the youngest of 5 daughters and 3

00:03:57.819 --> 00:03:58.819
sons.

00:03:58.819 --> 00:04:03.239
By the time that Konosuke was just 4 years
old, his father would end up losing their

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:07.550
entire family farm as he speculated on the
rice exchange.

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:13.000
Having lost everything, Konosuke’s father
would head to the city to find a job but that

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.170
wasn’t enough.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:19.620
Pretty soon, young Konosuke himself would
have to start working at just age 9.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:24.080
He would start off at a Hibachi store before
moving on to a Bicycle store, but while the

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.810
circumstances weren’t that great, these
jobs taught Konosuke a great deal about the

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:29.870
business world.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:34.910
How to attract customers, how to keep them
happy, how to keep employees loyal, and most

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:38.300
importantly, how to walk away with a modest
profit.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:43.880
During these long days of work, Konosuke would
start seeing streetcars pop up across Osaka

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:47.860
and this would convince him that the future
of business had to do with electricity.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:53.750
So, at age 15, he decided to leave his gig
at the bicycle shop and join the Osaka Electric

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:54.750
Light Company.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.880
As a natural hard worker, Konosuke would rapidly
rise up the ranks and become an inspector

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.150
by the time that he was just 22 years old.

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:08.000
It’s crazy to think that he already had
13 years of work experience, 7 of which were

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:09.389
in the electrical business by the time that
he was just 22.

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:14.490
All of this hard work had allowed Konosuke
to escape the depths of poverty but Konosuke

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:19.760
had far larger ambitions and this is when
he was start budding heads with management.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:25.210
In his early 20s for example, he would design
an improved light socked and pitch it to his

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:26.210
supervisor.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:30.970
But his supervisor would simply dismiss the
idea claiming that it was useless and would

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.220
go nowhere.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:37.600
Invigorated by these remarks, Konosuke would
decide to quit his job and try his hand at

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:38.600
business.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.800
And with that, he would set up shop inside
his tiny 2 room apartment with his wife and

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.650
brother in law in June of 1917.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:49.990
If this was a Dhar Mann video, Konosuke’s
new light socket would end up going viral

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:54.460
and he would end up buying out his old employer
and stick to to his old supervisor.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:06:00.030
But unfortunately, it’s not and unfortunately,
Konosuke’s light socket didn’t go anywhere

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:01.030
anywhere.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:04.759
In fact, things were so bad that he would
have to sell the few valuables that he had

00:06:04.759 --> 00:06:06.240
just to get by.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.880
Unwilling to quit though, Konosuke continued
experimenting and creating new products and

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.690
after several months he would finally land
on a product that someone actually wanted:

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:17.860
insulation plates for fans.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:22.830
This product gave him enough money to sustain
himself leading to the Matsushita Electric

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.729
aka Panasonic on March 7, 1918.

00:06:26.729 --> 00:06:31.280
But while things were going decently well
with the insulation plates, Konosuke still

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.690
didn’t have to heart to just give up on
the light sockets.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:38.630
So, he would revisit and redesign his socket
leading to the creation of 2 new products:

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:41.660
an attachment plug and a two way socket.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:45.830
Unlike Konosuke’s original light socket,
these new sockets would gain steam almost

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:52.770
immediately which brings us into Konosuke’s
first principle: always keep experimenting.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:56.450
Even if your first idea doesn’t work, it’s
more than likely that some iteration of it

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:57.450
would.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:02.950
In fact, Konosuke felt so strongly about this
that he would keep a pencil and paper by his

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:04.129
bedside.

00:07:04.129 --> 00:07:09.020
This way, if he got any new ideas in his sleep,
he could jot them down and put them to work.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:13.330
Anyway, the momentum of this new socket allowed
Konosuke to move his operation to a larger

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:18.729
two story house and scale his workforce to
20 employees, marking the beginning of Panasonic’s

00:07:18.729 --> 00:07:20.449
monumental run.

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:29.310
GREAT PRODUCTS DON’T SELL THEMSELVES:
Moving into the 1920s, things were going much

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.639
better.

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:34.660
Panasonic had turned into a legitimate local
business but that isn’t to say that the

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:36.740
road ahead was always smooth.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:41.530
In fact, Konosuke would find himself in another
difficult situation with his next product

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:42.530
itself.

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:46.780
What hurt the most with this product was that
going into it, Konosuke was sure that it was

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:48.520
gonna be a banger.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:53.120
It was one of those ideas that he got while
sleeping and woke up thinking: Eureka!

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:54.640
What was this product you ask?

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.530
Well, it was a simple bicycle light.

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:01.599
Battery powered lights were already a thing
but they were completely impractical as they

00:08:01.599 --> 00:08:03.870
only lasted 2 to 3 hours.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:09.500
So most people, including Konosuke himself
resorted to using a candle lamp when biking.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:13.930
Frustrated by this reality, Konosuke would
spend 6 months designing a bullet shaped bicycle

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:18.300
lamp that was truly revolutionary lasting
30 to 40 hours.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:22.419
Excited, Konosuke would strike deals with
a bunch of wholesalers and stock stores all

00:08:22.419 --> 00:08:28.590
throughout the city but to his utter disappointment,
not a soul would buy these lamps.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:30.010
Why you ask?

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:35.169
Well, because people had already made up their
mind that battery powered lamps sucked.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:39.440
Konosuke kept holding out hope that the strength
of product would shine through in this overlooked

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:42.529
market but it never happened.

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:48.570
To make things worse, the Great Kanto Earthquake
would strike in September of 1923 which destroyed

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:50.470
much of his sales partners.

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:55.510
But by now, I think you know that Konosuke
never quits. 4 years later, he would come

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:59.410
back to the bicycle lamp market with a square
shaped lamp.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:04.440
Nothing much had changed internally, but Konosuke’s
approach to selling these lamps was completely

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:05.440
different.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.730
For starters, the light was an odd square
shape, something that people had never seen

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:10.730
before.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:12.290
This was very much on purpose.

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:16.420
It was to emphasize that this light itself
was nothing like people had seen before.

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:21.470
Konosuke also bought out newspaper ads that
read "National Lamps: Buy for Your Assurance,

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:22.769
Use for Your Benefit."

00:09:22.769 --> 00:09:28.339
And to round it all off, Konosuke would hand
out 10,000 samples to stores without any sort

00:09:28.339 --> 00:09:29.579
of obligation.

00:09:29.579 --> 00:09:30.579
The result?

00:09:30.579 --> 00:09:34.810
Well, Konosuke would end up selling 30,000
units per month by the end of the first year

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:41.920
itself which brings us into Konosuke’s second
principle: good products don’t sell themselves.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.110
Just because you have a good product doesn’t
mean that people will line up outside your

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:46.870
door wanting to buy.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:51.240
Even a good product requires an even better
marketing campaign.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:55.440
Having realized this with the bicycle lamp,
Konosuke would vow to never let marketing

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.280
screw over a good product.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.980
And with that, he would completely restructure
the company in 1929.

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:06.620
He would create 4 succinct divisions, one
for each of their major product categories:

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:09.899
electrical sockets, batteries, bicycle lamps,
and radios.

00:10:09.899 --> 00:10:14.399
Each of these divisions had their own national
sales department with regional departments

00:10:14.399 --> 00:10:15.579
underneath them.

00:10:15.579 --> 00:10:20.209
And to top it off, Konosuke would directly
link the sales departments with their respective

00:10:20.209 --> 00:10:21.850
manufacturing departments.

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:27.470
This way, manufacturing was directly dependent
on sales, reducing the likelihood of over

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:28.829
or underproduction.

00:10:28.829 --> 00:10:34.130
With this brand new salesforce in place, Panasonic
would enter the 1930s with pure dominance

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:38.260
as they grew to be a national player employing
over 10,000 people.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:44.490
But, just as Panasonic was hitting a stride,
WW2 would roll around.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:53.570
DOWNTURNS ARE A BLESSING:
There’s really on easy way to put it, WW2

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:54.570
destroyed Panasonic.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:59.100
Throughout the war, Panasonic struggled to
get materials and hire talent as young men

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.670
were being recruited to the war.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:04.620
To make things worse, the company was forced
to focus on industrial production efforts

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:05.649
for the war.

00:11:05.649 --> 00:11:10.730
Konosuke was hopeful that things would get
better after the war but they only got worse

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:13.950
as I think we all know how the war came to
an end.

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:18.430
Japan was literally bombed into oblivion which
not only destroyed everything that Konosuke

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:22.270
had built but what the entire country had
built.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:26.250
Just when you thought things couldn’t get
any worse, General Douglas MacArthur and the

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:30.750
Americans would begin occupying the country
and basically take control.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:36.589
And one of their reform orders was for Konosuke
to step down and relinquish control of Panasonic.

00:11:36.589 --> 00:11:39.860
Konosuke didn’t even resist but you know
who did?

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:44.399
His 15,000 employees who signed a petition
to keep Konsuke in power.

00:11:44.399 --> 00:11:48.850
They argued that Konosuke was a virtal part
of their reconstruction effort and that removing

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:51.310
him would basically destroy the company.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:55.920
Fortunately for Panasonic, Konosuke’s resignation
order would end up getting rescinded allowing

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:58.019
Konosuke to continue leading the company.

00:11:58.019 --> 00:12:03.690
But, while Konosuke had somehow managed to
keep control of his company, he had lost all

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:04.880
momentum.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:10.959
Any normal person would’ve been distraught
beyond belief but Konosuke was never the pessimistic

00:12:10.959 --> 00:12:11.959
type.

00:12:11.959 --> 00:12:18.000
In fact, he was rather optimistic which brings
us into his 3rd core business principle.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.290
Good times are good and bad times are even
better.

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:25.800
You see, Konosuke believes that downturns
are the perfect time to evaluate the state

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:27.959
of a business and reinvent it.

00:12:27.959 --> 00:12:31.290
When things are going well, you’re usually
scared to do something different and screw

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:32.840
up what you already have.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:37.710
But, when things are going bad, well you’ve
got nothing left to lose so might as well

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:40.769
take a risk and see if that unlocks a whole
new level.

00:12:40.769 --> 00:12:43.240
And that’s exactly what Konosuke would do.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:46.910
He would tell his employees to approach the
business with the attitude that they had just

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:52.029
opened which wasn’t far from the truth given
that Panasonic would start targetting a completely

00:12:52.029 --> 00:12:54.540
different market: the global market.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:59.260
To be honest, Panasonic had no other choice
given that the Japanese economy was in no

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:02.139
state to support so many employees by themselves.

00:13:02.139 --> 00:13:06.970
So, the entire 1950s was focused on launching
western products.

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:12.360
This included Washing Machines, TVs, refrigerators,
radios, portable radios, rice cookers, tape

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.220
recorders, and ACs.

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:18.260
This was also around the time that the name
Panasonic actually came about.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:22.850
Given the new international focus, they needed
an easy English name and they would settle

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:24.290
on Panasonic.

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:28.820
Pan means universal and Sonic means sound,
so “Panasonic” was supposed to signify

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:32.579
the company bringing the sound of Japan to
the entire world.

00:13:32.579 --> 00:13:40.300
And this marked the beginning of Panasonic’s
golden era: the 60s, 70s, and 80s but as we

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:46.190
now know, this golden era wouldn’t last
forever.

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:54.241
THE BRUTAL FALL:
It’s sad to say but Panasonic’s peak basically

00:13:54.241 --> 00:13:58.350
aligns exactly with Konosuke’s death in
1989.

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:03.410
Eversince Konosuke died, it has only been
downhill for Panasonic and it’s no wonder

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:04.459
why.

00:14:04.459 --> 00:14:08.459
They basically completely forgot about the
principles that made the business successful

00:14:08.459 --> 00:14:09.800
in the first place.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:13.110
Take Konosuke’s principle of always keep
experimenting for example.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:18.180
Well, after Konosuke’s death, Panasonic
completely stopped experimenting and if you

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:21.680
don’t wanna believe me just take it from
Panasonic’s website itself.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:23.680
Here’s all the products they launched in
the 50s.

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:26.459
Here’s all the products they launched in
the 60s and 70s.

00:14:26.459 --> 00:14:30.060
Here’s all the products they launched in
the 80s and even 90s.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:33.200
And here’s all the products they launched
in the 2000s.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:38.670
Yeah, you’re not looking at that wrong,
there’s not even a single product listed

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:39.730
on there.

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:43.990
Their last notable product was way back in
98 when they launched digital TVs for the

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:44.990
US.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:49.509
You can only hit the ball out of the park
if you try swinging.

00:14:49.509 --> 00:14:53.029
Panasonic has not swung in 25 years.

00:14:53.029 --> 00:14:57.560
But not only has Panasonic shied away from
new products but they’ve also dropped the

00:14:57.560 --> 00:14:59.500
ball on their existing product lines.

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:03.420
You know how we talked about how there was
no pivotal moment for Panasonic.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:06.920
It wasn’t like their products just suddenly
sucked or their pricing went through the roof

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:08.610
or anything like that.

00:15:08.610 --> 00:15:12.370
The reality is that Panasonic products are
still quite great.

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:18.360
But, the thing to keep in mind is that great
products don’t sell themselves, and this

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:21.740
is perfectly exemplified by Samsung.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:26.500
Samsung ranks lower than Panasonic across
the board when it comes to product quality,

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:32.029
pricing, and customer service but Samsung
is worth over 10 times Panasonic.

00:15:32.029 --> 00:15:33.290
You know why?

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:37.490
Because Samsung knows how to market their
products whether it’s appliances, TVs, or

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:38.520
smartphones.

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:42.500
Seriously, when was the last time you saw
a Panasonic ad?

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:45.520
I bet the answer is too long ago to remember.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.990
Now, when was the last time you saw a Samsung
ad?

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:52.940
I bet the answer is too recently to remember.

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:57.410
Basically anytime you’re browsing the web
or watching a video or shopping or driving,

00:15:57.410 --> 00:15:59.790
you probably saw Samsung branding.

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:04.490
But you probably don’t even remember because
you’re so desensitized to it.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:10.050
With this sort of marketing discrepancy, it’s
no wonder why people choose Samsung or LG

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:12.620
when buying a new TV or new appliances.

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:14.860
Good products don’t sell themselves.

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:19.560
Panasonic’s competitors understand this,
Panasonic doesn’t.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:24.220
Something else that Panasonic doesn’t seem
to understand is that downturns can be a blessing.

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:29.060
Back in the 40s, Panasonic had lost basically
everything but they were able to make it work

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.930
by not losing hope and shifting gears.

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:35.960
The early 2000s would’ve been the perfect
time to do this again.

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:40.089
Imagine if they were the first person to jump
onto the smartphone trend instead of Samsung

00:16:40.089 --> 00:16:42.649
or if they were the ones to capitlize on OLED
technology.

00:16:42.649 --> 00:16:47.180
They could’ve made a name for themselves
in the modern tech era.

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:52.839
But instead, it seems that Panasonic has lost
hope which is likely the most disappointing

00:16:52.839 --> 00:16:54.510
part of all of this.

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:59.910
Whether it was the failed light socket or
the bicycle lamp or WW2, one thing that remained

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:03.779
constant was Konosuke’s optimism for the
future.

00:17:03.779 --> 00:17:07.740
Modern Panasonic on the other hand seems to
have lost all hope.

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:11.850
They’re not experimenting, marketing or
leveraging the downturn.

00:17:11.850 --> 00:17:16.260
It almost seems like they’ve already accepted
defeat and are just trying to hold on for

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:21.220
as long as possible and with this sort of
mindset, it’s no wonder why the writing

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:23.949
is on the wall for their bankruptcy.

00:17:23.949 --> 00:17:27.419
Another legacy giant that’s actually managing
make a turnaround is Nokia.

00:17:27.419 --> 00:17:32.990
Check out this video to learn more.

